# house wins
P=[("01","Miles are a product. You are not the customer.","Airlines do not give miles away, they sell them in bulk to banks. Delta collected roughly $7.4 billion from its card partner in 2024, larger than the airline's entire operating profit that year. American and United run the same play. When the planes were grounded they mortgaged the loyalty programs alongside everything else: $9 billion raised against SkyMiles and $6.8 billion against MileagePlus in 2020, then $10 billion against AAdvantage in March 2021.","Delta figure from its 2024 Form 10-K. Loyalty financings from the 2020 and 2021 offering documents."),
("02","They win on the spread.","Programs sell miles to the banks for somewhere between 1.25 and 2 cents each, and independent valuations put typical redemption value at 1.1 to 1.5. Neither number appears in any filing, because co-brand pricing is confidential, but the direction of the gap is the business model and it repeats across every mile issued. In the 2019 figures the airlines showed bond investors, AAdvantage ran a 49 percent EBITDA margin and MileagePlus 34. The airlines themselves earned operating margins of 4.8 to 9.7 percent in 2024.","Margins from the 2019 AAdvantage and MileagePlus investor presentations."),
("03","They win again when you forget.","Points earned and never used have a name on the balance sheet: breakage. McKinsey estimated in 2018 that more than 30 trillion frequent flyer miles were sitting unredeemed, and it is still the most-cited figure in the industry. Breakage typically runs a quarter to a third of everything issued, around 25 percent in retail programs and 15 to 30 in airline ones, and every one of those forgotten points is margin nobody has to deliver. Your balance is in that number.","Breakage and unredeemed miles from McKinsey, 2018. See the Ghost Economy on The Daily Redemption for the full working."),
("04","One move beats the house.","Redeem above the spread. Move the points into the airline or hotel program itself, at the right moment, against the right chart, on dates that actually have space, and the value lands on your side of the table instead of theirs. That takes somebody watching all day, every day. That is the entire job you are hiring.","")]
